Both commits are fit to merge. They merge cleanly into `jusprin-newui` and with each other. Details below, with the fix's source diff reproduced since that is the behaviour change.

## Tool-record fix, cc5c12f9ff on `claude/tool-record-terminal-0b61d2`

**What it changes.** The save that follows a model edit is now part of the call's outcome. The coordinator announces the outcome once, after the save. A failed save reaches the model as `failed / local_save_failed` with the existing "do not repeat the call" message, and the stored record says the same thing. Before, the model heard "succeeded" and was never told about the failure, because the continuation had already been consumed.

```diff
--- a/src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/Agent/ToolExecutionCoordinator.cpp
+++ b/src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/Agent/ToolExecutionCoordinator.cpp
@@ -1097,25 +1097,41 @@ void ToolExecutionCoordinator::pump()
             // A tool that replaces the project clears the other activities
             // while it runs, which may move this one: read it before.
-            const ActionClass action_class = activity.action_class;
+            const bool document_boundary = !versioned && m_document_boundary && activity.action_class != ActionClass::ReadOnly &&
+                                           definition != nullptr && definition->handler != ToolHandler::ProjectOpen &&
+                                           definition->handler != ToolHandler::CalibrationGenerate &&
+                                           definition->handler != ToolHandler::SliceStart;
+            // The save after a change is part of its outcome. Subscribers,
+            // the model's tool result among them, are told that outcome once,
+            // after the save, so a failed save is what they hear and the
+            // record keeps what they heard.
+            struct Saved { std::string& id; ~Saved() { id.clear(); } } saved{m_saving_after};
+            if (versioned || document_boundary)
+                m_saving_after = activity.action_id;
             execute(activity);
+            bool save_failed = false;
             if (versioned) {
                 try {
                     m_version_after(activity, before);
                 } catch (const std::exception& error) {
                     std::clog << "Could not save after Agent action: " << error.what() << '\n';
-                    fail(activity, "local_save_failed", "The change was made, but ...");
+                    save_failed = true;
                 }
-            } else if (m_document_boundary && action_class != ActionClass::ReadOnly && definition != nullptr &&
-                       definition->handler != ToolHandler::ProjectOpen && ...) {
+            } else if (document_boundary) {
                 try {
                     m_document_boundary(activity);
                 } catch (const std::exception& error) {
                     std::clog << "Could not save Agent document: " << error.what() << '\n';
-                    fail(activity, "local_save_failed", "The change was made, but ...");
+                    save_failed = true;
                 }
             }
+            if (save_failed)
+                fail(activity, "local_save_failed", "The change was made, but the project could not be saved. Do not repeat the call; tell the person the project is not saved.");
+            if (!m_saving_after.empty()) {
+                m_saving_after.clear();
+                if (tool_state_terminal(activity.state))
+                    notify(activity);
+            }
         }
@@ -2395,6 +2411,10 @@ void ToolExecutionCoordinator::fail(...)
+    // Terminal records never change once announced; only a change whose save
+    // is still to come has not been announced yet.
+    if (tool_state_terminal(activity.state) && activity.action_id != m_saving_after)
+        throw std::logic_error("Tool call " + activity.action_id + " already ended; its outcome cannot change");
     activity.state = ToolState::Failed;
@@ -2402,6 +2422,9 @@ void ToolExecutionCoordinator::notify(const ToolActivity& activity)
 {
+    // pump() announces this outcome once the save after it is done.
+    if (!m_saving_after.empty() && activity.action_id == m_saving_after && tool_state_terminal(activity.state))
+        return;
```

Plus one member, `m_saving_after`, in the header. Full diff is in the worktree at [ToolExecutionCoordinator.cpp](.claude/worktrees/tool-record-terminal-0b61d2/src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/Agent/ToolExecutionCoordinator.cpp:1097).

**My assessment.**

- **Correct and in the right layer.** The coordinator owns the outcome, and the fix decides it once. No guard was added in the host.
- **The throw in `fail()` is safe.** I checked every `fail()` call site in the file, about seventy. All are on a freshly submitted record or a running activity. None can hit an announced record, so the throw only fires on a real invariant break, where the project's rule says it should reach Sentry.
- **The version-after callback can't alter the record.** It takes the activity by const reference, so deferring the announcement past it changes nothing about what subscribers see besides the outcome itself.
- **One smell, not blocking.** The filter in `notify()` means a general function knows about `pump()`'s state. And the scope guard plus the explicit clear both reset the same field. The guard covers a throw from `execute()`, the explicit clear lets the one announcement through. It works, but one local flag would read more simply. I'd merge as is and not spend a round trip on it.
- **Tests.** The bridge test reproduces the model receiving "succeeded" on HEAD, with six failing assertions, and checks the next turn replays the same outcome, which is exactly the handoff's 4.9 concern. The coordinator test covers both save paths and asserts exactly one terminal notification. 463 cases pass.
- **Left out.** Seven MCP bridge cases hang on HEAD too. Both sessions reported this independently. It is a pre-existing problem worth its own look, not this commit's.

## Guard test, 378b3037a0 on `claude/cache-invariant-test-51dab5`

Two hidden Catch2 cases tagged `[.][history-prefix]`, 294 lines, all in [test_agent_bridge.cpp](.claude/worktrees/cache-invariant-test-51dab5/tests/agent/test_agent_bridge.cpp:5432). Each drives the real host, loop and codec against a recording transport that keeps every request body as posted and answers from a script, with signed thinking on Anthropic and encrypted reasoning items on Responses.

**How it compares.** Each body is parsed with order preserved and must re-serialise to itself, so every part is the bytes the service received. The prompt is split into parts in the provider's order: tools, system, messages for Anthropic; tools, instructions, input for Responses. Turn N+1's first request must open with every part of turn N's last request, in order, and add to it. Every other top-level field must be identical. The first differing part is printed whole.

**The one exclusion** is Anthropic's moving `cache_control` breakpoint on the newest user content. The comment cites the docs for why a marker is not part of the cached content. The tool and system breakpoints stay in the comparison.

**Fail-first evidence.** On HEAD both cases fail on both codecs. The reported first differences independently confirm four of the handoff's claims: turn 2 differs at the first message, which is replayed without its workspace block; turn 3 differs where the app note is missing; the skills case fails at the file-load reminder appearing in front of message 0; and on Responses the slice-failed skill is dropped. With the prototype applied, the main case passes and the skills case fails exactly where predicted.

One consequence for D2 I had not seen: without the note, two consecutive assistant replies merge into one message on Anthropic. So "never send notes" is not a neutral choice. It changes the shape of the history.

**Two small things, neither blocking.** Only the turn boundary is compared, not requests within a turn. Within-turn appending is what the codecs already do, so it's a cheap addition later rather than a gap now. And because the cases are hidden, CTest never runs them. The step that makes them pass must un-hide them. I'll add that to R7 in the plan doc.

